#original-page(612pt, 792pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.977pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(303pt, 739.56pt, "2 ")
  #line-at(54pt, 65.16pt, "integrate high-k crystalline nanomembranes with GaN with minimal damage to the interface")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.025pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(489.6pt, 60.6pt, "25,26")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.977pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(507.84pt, 65.16pt, ". Recently, ")
  #line-at(54pt, 85.8pt, "J. Ji et al. integrated crystalline STO on AlGaN/GaN HEMT by nanomembrane transfer method and ")
  #line-at(54pt, 106.56pt, "utilized it as gate dielectric, achieved near-theoretical subthreshold swing (62 mV/dec) and high on-off ")
  #line-at(54pt, 127.2pt, "ratio (2.6 ×10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.025pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(118.92pt, 122.64pt, "7")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.977pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(123pt, 127.2pt, ")")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.025pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(126.96pt, 122.64pt, "27")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.977pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(135.12pt, 127.2pt, ". However, the detailed electrical characterization of crystalline STO nanomembrane-on-")
  #line-at(54pt, 147.96pt, "GaN such as dielectric constant (k), C-V (capacitance-voltage) characteristics, leakage current, frequency ")
  #line-at(54pt, 168.6pt, "dispersion etc. are not available in literature. In this work, we integrated high-quality, high-k (k>115) ")
  #line-at(54pt, 189.36pt, "crystalline STO nanomembrane (50 nm and 200 nm) on GaN by nanomembrane transfer method and ")
  #line-at(54pt, 210pt, "reported the detailed electrical characteristics. In our fabricated STO membrane there was no leakage ")
  #line-at(54pt, 230.76pt, "current (< noise-level of the tool) up to 6V and 11V for 50 nm and 200 nm membrane respectively, the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 251.4pt, "dielectric constant was >50 (50 nm) and >115 (200 nm). There was no frequency dispersion of capacitance ")
  #line-at(54pt, 272.16pt, "and dielectric constant between 10 kHz and 500kHz, the capacitance value was almost perfectly ")
  #line-at(54pt, 292.8pt, "proportional to the area of the contact pad- indicating the high-quality of the dielectric. STO was chosen ")
  #line-at(54pt, 313.56pt, "as the dielectric material because of its high bulk dielectric constant (k>300), optical transparency in the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 334.2pt, "visible range (for potential applications in optical devices) and excellent chemical and thermal stability")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.025pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(540.12pt, 329.64pt, "28,29")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.977pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(558.12pt, 334.2pt, ".   ")
  #line-at(54pt, 375.6pt, "The growth and transfer process of the nanomembrane is depicted in Fig. 1. The membrane (50nm and ")
  #line-at(54pt, 396.36pt, "200nm) was grown on 10nm SCAO (Scandium Aluminum Oxide, ScAlO")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.025pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(407.28pt, 397.32pt, "3")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.977pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(411.36pt, 396.36pt, ")/ bulk STO (001) substrate by ")
  #line-at(54pt, 417pt, "pulsed laser deposition (PLD) at 750°C. The 10nm SCAO layer was almost lattice matched to STO and ")
  #line-at(54pt, 437.76pt, "water soluble. Following membrane growth, platinum (Pt) electrodes were sputtered on the membrane as ")
  #line-at(54pt, 458.4pt, "contact pads (pad diameter = 50 μm and 100 μm). Subsequently, the sample was immersed in deionized ")
  #line-at(54pt, 479.16pt, "(DI) water, resulting in the separation of the membrane from the bulk material. The detached membrane ")
  #line-at(54pt, 499.8pt, "then floated on the water surface. Thereafter, a 2 μm n+ GaN / 2 μm UID GaN/20 nm AlGaN/ sapphire ")
  #line-at(54pt, 520.56pt, "substrate (PSS) sample was dipped in the water and was gradually raised above the surface so that floating ")
  #line-at(54pt, 541.2pt, "membrane clung to its n+ GaN face. Following that, ohmic contacts to the n+ GaN were made. Then, the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 561.96pt, "membrane was annealed in the atmosphere at 250 °C for two hours. The membrane was then electrically ")
  #line-at(54pt, 582.6pt, "characterized, both before and after annealing.  ")
  #line-at(72pt, 603.36pt, " After transfer, there were some cracks on some part of the membrane however there was enough ")
  #line-at(54pt, 624pt, "“clean” surface as well (> 50%) to fabricate devices (Fig. 2). The surface of the STO membrane was found ")
  #line-at(54pt, 644.76pt, "to be atomically smooth in AFM images (Fig. 2). XRD measurements showed the existence of single-")
  #line-at(54pt, 665.4pt, "crystalline STO (200) and STO (100) peaks as depicted in Fig. 3. The leakage current through the ")
  #line-at(54pt, 686.16pt, "membrane was characterized using IV measurements in a B1505A SMU, as presented in Fig. 4.. The ")
  #line-at(54pt, 706.8pt, "reverse leakage through the membrane was below noise-level up to -7 V and -14 V for 50 nm and 200 nm ")
]

